n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUntitled - Willcox (https://willcox.az.gov/s/2026-03-19-AMENDED-Special-City-Council-Packet.pdf)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Mar 19, 2026 — Data centers provide a substantial amount of money. We'll hear people say there isn't enough energy. All they use for us is pass-through, it ...Read more--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA lot of concerns have been raised about data centers. ... (https://www.facebook.com/CochiseCountyAZ/videos/-a-lot-of-concerns-have-been-raised-about-data-centers-yesterday-the-board-didnt/1262852899322071/)\nciteturn3search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Arizona's data center boom is raising important questions a... Aug 21, 2026 · 215 views. Charlotte County leaders are meeting Tuesday to ...\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCivicPlus.CMS.FAQ - Cochise County - az.gov (https://cochise.az.gov/faq.aspx?TID=53)\nciteturn3search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; This program is available to property owners of parcels that are at least 4-acres in size with the following zoning designations: RU (RU-4, RU-10, RU-18, ...Read more\n### \n\nSkip to Main Content\n\n[Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\n\nCreate a Website Account - Manage notification subscriptions, save form progress and more.   \n\n  * Website Sign In\n\n[Input][Input]\n\n[Input: Search...] Search\n\n  1. Home\n  2. FAQ\n\n[Input: Search and discover questions]\n\n[Select]\n\n## Building Safety Division\n\n18\n\n  * ### [Button: Am in the right zoning to participate in Cochise County Owner Builder Program? ]\n\nThis program is available to property owners of parcels that are at least 4-acres in size with the following zoning designations:\n\n    * RU (RU-4, RU-10, RU-18, or RU-36 only)\n    * SM (SM-174, SM-10, SM-18, or SM-36 only)\n    * SR (SR-174, SR-10, SR-18, or SR-36 only)\n\n You can check your property's zoning on the County's interactive web map ArcGIS Cochise County INFOMap. Search by address or parcel number/APN (Format: 123459876). \n\nBuilding Safety Division\n\n  * ### [Button: Do I need any other permits if I participate in the Cochise County Owner Builder Program? ]\n\nYes, you will be required to obtain a septic system, or alternative waste treatment, permit. While these are issued and inspected by the County Health Department, you may still apply for a septic permit through our online permitting system: Register and apply for a permit online. Please contact the County Health Department if you have specific questions regarding a septic or alternative system to treat waste. Phone: 520-432-9441, Email: envhealth@cochise.az.gov\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUnpopular tipping fees that were instituted last July for Nye ... (https://www.facebook.com/KPVM25/posts/unpopular-tipping-fees-that-were-instituted-last-july-for-nye-county-residents-h/1655985183201496/)\nciteturn3search5 [wordlim: 200] Now DATA CENTERS!!!!! 10w · 6 likes. Lorraine Gilbert. This is a positive step up for many but our landfill is at near compacity. 10w · 7 likes.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAssessor | Cochise County, AZ (https://www.cochise.az.gov/178/Assessor)\nciteturn3search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; The Cochise County Assessor's Office has the responsibility to locate, identify and value all locally assessable property within Cochise County at fair ...\n# Assessor\n\nThe Cochise County Assessor's Office has the responsibility to locate, identify and value all locally assessable property within Cochise County at fair market value as defined by Arizona State Constitution and Title 42 of the Arizona Revised Statutes. The office maintains current ownership records and property assessment maps. Exemptions for qualifying organizations and individuals are processed annually as are Senior Property Valuation Protection Option applications.\n\n  * Find more details about the Arizona Property Assessment Process\n\n  *   *   * ## Related Links\n\n  * Treasurer's Office\n  * GIS Maps\n  * Development Services\n\n### Assessor Office Locations and Hours\n\n  1. #### Bisbee Office\n\nPhysical Address [Button: View Map]\n1415 Melody Lane\nBuilding B\nBisbee, AZ 85603\n\n1415 Melody Lane Building B Bisbee AZ 85603\n\nDirections\n\nPhone: 520-432-8650\n\nFax: 520-432-8698\n\nHours\n\nMonday through Friday\n8 am to 5 pm\n\n  1. #### Benson Satellite Office\n\nPhysical Address [Button: View Map]\n126 W 5th Street\nSuite 7\nBenson, AZ 85602\n\n126 W 5th Street Suite 7 Benson AZ 85602\n\nDirections\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nsigns being taken down in putnam county | Lake City, GA (https://www.facebook.com/groups/taxpayerwatchdoggroupputnamcountyga/posts/1600729498354431/)\nciteturn3search8 [wordlim: 200] I know we need government in Putnam….but when I see taking others signs down and replaced with your own that your honesty?!?!?!?!--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCochise County will regulate data centers, but some ... (https://news.azpm.org/s/103110-cochise-county-will-regulate-data-centers-but-some-residents-want-more/)\nciteturn3search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Apr 10, 2026 — Antenori said he expects Cochise County to receive a data center application by 2028. To the west, the City of Tucson and Pima County did not ...\nMany suggestions raised by the public were related to water. The county’s new regulations bar data centers from using drinking water for cooling purposes unless other water is “not reasonably available or feasible.” Cheryl Knott argued they create a loophole for companies to use groundwater and drinking water.\n\n“Data centers should only be allowed on land where they will be replacing an existing operation that uses more water than the data center will,” she said, calling it a net-zero water use policy.\n\nData centers have traditionally used large amounts of water to cool their servers. Recently, many data center developers, including those planning Project Blue in Pima County, have transitioned to air-cooled or closed-loop water systems. Antenori said he wants data centers in Cochise County to use cooling systems that don’t require large amounts of water.\n\nAnother commenter, Shana Gall, encouraged the county to sign agreements with potential developers that would require them to pay into well-digging funds and purchase county bonds held in escrow until they are needed for decommissioning the data center.\n\n“Growth must fix what is broken. We must act as stewards. We cannot allow these structures to become the abandoned mines of the digital age,” she said.\n\nThe county’s new regulations outline steps for declaring unused data centers abandoned and decommissioning them and state the county \"may require\" owners to pay for that process. Antenori suggested asking potential data center developers to help Cochise County buy hazmat trucks for local fire departments, citing a recent train derailment.\n\nAntenori said he expects Cochise County to receive a data center application by 2028. To the west, the City of Tucson and Pima County did not have specialized data center regulations in place when the Project Blue development was approved in June 2025. This week, Tucson city leaders reviewed potential new data center restrictions. Pima County’s code does not contain any special restrictions concerning data centers.\n\nMORE:\n\nDATA CENTERS ECONOMY ENVIRONMENT\n\nYOU MAY ALSO LIKE: Tucson approves new regulations for large-scale data centers DOE awards UA $1.7 million for mining research U.S. Forest Service proposes removing a rule that prohibits road construction and logging in national forests\n\nBy posting comments, you agree to our [Button: Comment Policy]\n\nAZPM encourages comments, but comments that contain profanity, unrelated information, threats, libel, defamatory statements, obscenities, pornography or that violate the law are not allowed. Comments that promote commercial products or services are not allowed. Comments in violation of this policy will be removed. Continued posting of comments that violate this policy will result in the commenter being banned from the site.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDepartments | Cochise County, AZ (https://www.cochise.az.gov/101/Departments)\nciteturn3search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; The Cochise County Assessor's Office has the responsibility to locate, identify and value all locally assessable property within Cochise County at fair ...Read more\n### \n\nSkip to Main Content\n\n[Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\n\nCreate a Website Account - Manage notification subscriptions, save form progress and more.   \n\n  * Website Sign In\n\n[Input][Input]\n\n[Input: Search...] Search\n\n  1. Home\n  2. Departments\n\n[Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\n\n# Departments\n\n### Cochise County Telephone Directory - click to open\n\nWelcome to Cochise County - this page contains an alphabetical listing of our Departments.\n\n  1. Assessor\n\nThe Cochise County Assessor's Office has the responsibility to locate, identify and value all locally assessable property within Cochise County at fair market value as defined by Arizona State Constitution and Title 42 of the Arizona Revised Statutes.\n\n  2. Board of Supervisors\n\nThe Board of Supervisors is the governing and policy-making body of the Cochise County.\n\n  3. Clerk of the Board\n\nDiscover the Clerk of the Board's taxation, licenses, and contact information.\n\n  4. Constable\n\nOur primary function is to serve processes from Arizona and other court systems. Our purpose is to provide courteous and professional service in a timely and diligent manner. Our goal is to accomplish our objectives while meeting and exceeding the standards of trust and integrity the public deserves.\n\n  5. County Administration\n\nAccess information about County Administration.\n\n  6. County Attorney\n\nAccess information about the Cochise County Attorney's Office.\n\n  7. Court Services\n\nCheck out more information on the Cochise County court system.\n\n  8. Development Services\n\nWelcome to the Development Services Department We're committed to enhancing residents' quality of life by ensuring our County is the ideal place to live, work, and visit. If you have any questions related to planning or development, feel free to contact us today.\n\n  9. Elections\n\nThe Elections Department prepares, administers, and conducts federal, state, and local elections in accordance with, and not limited to, the Arizona Revised Statutes, the Secretary of State's Procedure Manual, the Help America Vote Act (HAVA), and other applicable laws and regulations.\n\n  10. Emergency Management\n\nWe are fortunate to have committed fire, law enforcement, emergency medical, and public works agencies throughout the 6,200 square miles of this county.\n\n  11. Health & Social Services\n\nThe Mission of Cochise Health & Social Services is to foster exceptional quality of life by advocating for a community-centered culture of health through unparalleled public health service.\n\n  12. Housing Authority\n\nThe Housing Authority of Cochise County (HACC) was established by the Cochise County Board of Supervisors.\n\n  13. Human Resources\n\nCochise County Human Resources is dedicated to serving as a resource for all employees, both current and future.\n\n  14. Indigent Defense\n\nThe Cochise County Public Defender, Legal Defender, and Office of Legal Advocate have collectively been assisting and defending the Accused in Cochise County for well over twenty years.\n\n  15. Internal Services\n\nThe Internal Services Department provides a wide range of services and operational support to the County of Cochise organization.\n\n  16. Library\n\nVisit the Cochise County Library District website to search the library catalog, access ebooks and research tools, and find more information about the libraries and library services.\n\n  17. Procurement\n\nDiscover information about Cochise County Procurement including procurement opportunities, auctions, vendor registration, and more!\n\n  18. Public Works\n\nThe Public Works Department is responsible for highways, solid waste, and more.\n\n  19. Recorder\n\nThe County Recorder performs two essential functions: processing documents primarily related to real estate transactions and maintaining voter registration records for all jurisdictions within the county and performing related responsibilities as statutorily required.\n\n  20. School Superintendent\n\nWithin the scope of the Arizona Revised Statutes and in collaboration with state and local agencies, the Cochise County School Superintendent’s office provides guidance, advocacy, programs, and services which support Cochise County Schools.\n\n  21. Sheriff\n\nFind information about the Sheriff's Department.\n\n  22. Treasurer\n\nThe Cochise County Treasurer is the fiscal custodian of the County and is responsible for the administration and control of cash and securities.\n\n  23. SEACOM 911\n\nSEACOM has the responsibility to answer 911 and non-emergency calls for assigned areas of Cochise County.\n\n### Quick Links\n\n  1. Bisbee Melody Lane Service Center Map (PDF)\n\n  2. Map of our County Service Centers\n\n  3. Customer Satisfaction Form\n\n  4. County Department Directory\n\nView All\n\n  * Agendas & Minutes\n\n  * Employment\n\n  * Pay Online\n\n  * Permits\n\n  * Notifications\n\n  * Vital Records\n\n  1.   *   *   *   *   * ### Contact Us\n\n  1. County Offices\n\n  2. 1415 Melody Lane, Building G\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBuilding and zoning in Benzonia for property development (https://www.facebook.com/groups/139884926723561/posts/1252021738843202/)\nciteturn3search11 [wordlim: 200] For instance, there are questions about \"dark skies,\" data centers, farmland, solar farms, short-term rentals, workforce housing ...Read more--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCochise County - Government (https://www.facebook.com/CochiseCountyAZ/posts/-at-the-board-of-supervisors-in-bisbee-this-weeks-regular-meeting-will-start-at-/1395245219313602/)\nciteturn3search12 [wordlim: 200] LOOKING AHEAD There are currently no active data center applications in Cochise County. With Ordinance 26-09 in place, the County is positioned to evaluate any ...--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCivicPlus.CMS.FAQ - Cochise County - az.gov (https://www.cochise.az.gov/faq.aspx?TID=33)\nciteturn3search13 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Easement disputes can be complex. Development Services is generally not involved in civil easement disputes. We recommend contacting a title company\nDevelopment Services\n\n  * ### [Button: Can you tell me the location of easements on or near my property? ]\n\nDevelopment Services does not maintain records of easements on individual lots that are not part of recorded subdivisions. Recorded subdivisions may have easement information listed as part of the recorded plat. However, even these may not be the most up-to-date. In most circumstances, we recommend contacting a surveyor, real estate attorney, or title company for a title report. Specifically, the Schedule B portion of a title report lists the encumbrances and exceptions, including easements, that affect a property, and will contain the most current and accurate information. You can also search for recorded documents online through the thecountyrecorder.com.\n\nDevelopment Services\n\n  * ### [Button: How do I resolve an easement dispute with my neighbor? ]\n\nEasement disputes can be complex. Development Services is generally not involved in civil easement disputes. We recommend contacting a title company to conduct a full title search. You may also consider hiring a surveyor to physically locate and mark your property boundaries as reflected in your deed. In some cases, you may benefit from consulting an experienced real estate attorney to analyze your specific situation and help you better understand your rights. \n\nDevelopment Services\n\n  * ### [Button: How much does a permit cost? ]\n\nAll permit fees are included in the Development Services Fee Schedule. We also have online, interactive permit fee calculators to help you estimate your specific project costs. \n\n    * Scroll to the bottom of this page to find our Residential Permit Fee Calculator. \n    * Scroll to the bottom of this page to find our Commercial/Non-Residential Permit Fee Calculator\n\nDevelopment Services\n\n  * ### [Button: How do I apply for a permit? ]\n\nBuilding permits are submitted online through the County’s permitting portal. Create an account, complete the application, and upload the required plans and supporting documents. Once your application is submitted, you can track its status and receive notifications as it moves through the review process. If you need assistance or are unsure which permit you need, Building Safety staff are available to help in person at the Bisbee office Monday through Friday, 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m.\n\nRegister and apply for a permit online.\n\nDevelopment Services\n\n  * ### [Button: Am I allowed to have livestock (goats, horses, pigs...) on my property? ]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCochise County sets guidelines for data centers (https://www.kold.com/2026/04/14/cochise-county-sets-guidelines-data-centers/)\nciteturn3search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Apr 13, 2026 — The board defines a data center as a facility that hosts, processes, or transmits data, and includes the need for cooling, power, and security.\n##### Developers would be required to submit comprehensive proposal\n\nData centers are being built everywhere, and Arizona is no exception.\n\nBy Renee Romo\n\nPublished: Apr. 13, 2026 at 9:55 PM MST|Updated: Apr. 13, 2026 at 9:56 PM MST\n\nShare\n\nAdd Us On Google\n\nTUCSON, Ariz. (13 News) - Data centers are being built everywhere, and Arizona is no exception.\n\nCochise County is now joining the discussion.\n\nThe rapid development of data centers has prompted the Cochise County Board of Supervisors to prepare for that possibility closer to home.\n\nTo do that, the board made some changes to zoning laws, specifically through an ordinance, to prevent any future data centers from becoming a problem.\n\nThe board defines a data center as a facility that hosts, processes, or transmits data, and includes the need for cooling, power, and security.\n\nChairman of the board, Frank Antenori, said the move to define and regulate data centers needed to be done.\n\n“If a data center had submitted a permit request before we adopted this ordinance, there would have been very little to stop them,” Antenori said.\n\nThe ordinance requires any interested developers to submit a comprehensive proposal, including a site plan, a breakdown of how the project would utilize local infrastructure, a noise study, a fire emergency plan, and a water use plan.\n\nBenson resident Kimberly Skolkim said the area already struggles with water rights, so the last thing the community needs is a data center coming in.\n\n“We need to prevent anything that is a large water guzzler from superseding the usage of the people that are already here,” Skolkim said.\n\nAntenori insisted that this current ordinance is just a starting point and that they will continue to add to it, including possible limitations on water and power usage.\n\nHe said there will be a work session where they will go over public input on what should be added to strengthen the ordinance.\n\n“Then we’ll have what would be the somewhat permanent ordinance in place that would be what we would work off when we do receive an application,” Antenori said.\n\nWhen it comes to power usage, Sulphur Springs Valley Electric Cooperative CEO Jason Bowling said their goal is always to ensure safe, reliable, and affordable service.\n\nSo, if a data center were to come to Cochise County, they would ensure it’s done in a way that benefits everyone.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWho to contact in Cochise County for property access (https://www.facebook.com/groups/509814449963302/posts/1802450837366317/)\nciteturn3search15 [wordlim: 200] Looking for recommendations on who to contact at the county regarding how to gain access to my property. I’m able to get onto it by 4x4 but can’t move ...--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArizona AG's proposed data center pause: impact on ... (https://www.facebook.com/cochiselivelocal/posts/arizona-ag-calls-for-data-center-pause-what-does-it-mean-for-cochise-county%EF%B8%8Fcoch/1644995174303426/)\nciteturn3search16 [wordlim: 200] LOOKING AHEAD There are currently no active data center applications in Cochise County. With Ordinance 26-09 in place, the County is positioned ...--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDevelopment Services | Cochise County, AZ (https://www.cochise.az.gov/202/Development-Services)\nciteturn3search17 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Cochise County's Development Services Department oversees permitting, zoning, building code compliance, and land-use planning for areas outside of city limits. ...\nView All\n\n  1. Home\n  2. Departments\n  3. Development Services\n\n[Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\n\n# Development Services\n\n[Input] [Input] [Input]\n\n### Permit & Planning Applications\n\n### Development Services Fees\n\n### Development Maps & Spatial Info\n\n### Search Our Files (Permits, Violations)\n\n## Welcome to the Development Services Department \n\nCochise County's Development Services Department oversees permitting, zoning, building code compliance, and land-use planning for areas outside of city limits. Each application is carefully reviewed to ensure it meets county requirements, helping development projects to proceed smoothly and with confidence.\n\n  * Comprehensive Plan (PDF)\n  * Zoning Regulations (link)\n  * Subdivision Regulations (link)\n  * Building Safety Regulations (link)\n  * Development Services Fee Schedule (link)\n\n### BUILDING SAFETY DIVISION\n\nThe Building Safety Division coordinates all facets of building plan review and the subsequent inspection process during residential and commercial construction to ensure code conformance in the interest of public health, safety, and welfare. \n\n  * Online Building Permit Applications\n  * Residential Plan Requirements Checklist (PDF)\n  * Building Permit Review Timelines (PDF)\n  * Request a Pre-Application Meeting with County Staff\n\n### CODE COMPLIANCE DIVISION\n\nThe Code Compliance Division enforces local ordinances to ensure properties meet zoning standards. They investigate complaints, conduct inspections, and work with property owners to achieve voluntary compliance. When necessary, they may issue citations or pursue legal action to address violations. Their goal is to maintain community standards and enhance quality of life.\n\n  * Report a Violation\n\n### PLANNING & ZONING DIVISION\n\nThe Planning and Zoning Division is responsible for long and short-range planning in the unincorporated areas. This Division oversees the administration of all land use regulations, which includes the County's zoning and subdivision regulations.\n\n  * Online Planning Applications\n  * Planning Application Review Timeframes (Administrative) (PDF)\n  * Public Hearings Comment Form\n  * Speaker Request Form (Planning & Zoning Commission and Board of Adjustment only)\n  * Request a Pre-Application Meeting with County Staff\n\n### BOARD AND HEARING OFFICER SUPPORT\n\nIn addition to daily permitting, code compliance, and planning functions, Development Services provides administrative and staff support to the following boards and hearing bodies:\n\nBoard of Adjustment\nThe Board of Adjustment is a quasi-judicial body that hears appeals of administrative zoning decisions and considers variances when unique property conditions create practical difficulties in meeting zoning requirements.\n\nBuilding Code Advisory and Appeals Board\nA Building Code Advisory and Appeals Board is technical board that reviews appeals related to building code interpretations and considers alternative materials, methods, or designs to ensure safe, code-compliant construction. \n\nPlanning and Zoning Commission\nThe Planning and Zoning Commission is a citizen commission that reviews rezoning requests, special use permits, comprehensive plan amendments, and other land-use proposals, and provides recommendations to the Board of Supervisors.\n\nHearing Officer\nThe Hearing Officer conducts administrative hearings for alleged code compliance violations. Property owners and County staff may present evidence and testimony, and the Hearing Officer issues decisions based on County ordinances and applicable regulations.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCity of Casa Grande - Government (https://www.facebook.com/cityofcg/posts/city-council-recap-from-the-august-3-2026-council-meetingthe-casa-grande-city-co/1557492309751606/)\nciteturn3search18 [wordlim: 200] LOOKING AHEAD There are currently no active data center applications in Cochise County. With Ordinance 26-09 in place, the County is positioned ...--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRecorder (https://www.cochise.az.gov/498/Recorder)\nciteturn3search19 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; The County Recorder performs two essential functions: processing documents primarily related to real estate transactions and maintaining voter registration ...\n### FAQs\n\n  1. Who can register to vote?\n  2. How do I register to vote?\n  3. Where are the voter registration forms available?\n\nView All\n\n  1. Home\n  2. Departments\n  3. Recorder\n\n[Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\n\n# Recorder\n\nThe County Recorder performs two essential functions: processing documents primarily related to real estate transactions and maintaining voter registration records for all jurisdictions within the county and performing related responsibilities as statutorily required.\n\n## Early Ballot Drop-Off Locations\n\nOn Election day, you may drop off your early ballot at any polling location from 6:00 am to 7:00 pm.\n\n  * Vew Election Day Polling Locations (PDF)\n\n## Document Search\n\n  * Search for Recorded Documents\n\n## Recording Notification\n\n  * Sign up for Recording Notification Service\n\n  1. Recording Requirements\n  2. Electronic Recording\n  3. Recorder History\n\n  1. Recording Requirements\n\n## Recording Requirements\n\nArizona Revised Statutes (ARS) 44-1373\n\nPursuant to ARS 11-480:\n\n  1. Electronic Recording\n\n## Electronic Recording\n\nThe Cochise County Recorder's Office has been providing Electronic Recording which is much faster and more streamlined than paper recording per the Arizona Revised State Statute (ARS) Section 11-487 (PDF).\n\n### Advantages of E-Recording\n\n  * Reduced labor, materials, postage and courier costs\n  * Shortened time gap to recordation\n  * Fewer document errors and rejections\n  * Increased security in sending and receiving documents\n  * Improved tracking and filing of documents\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCochise County adopts ordinance to regulate data centers after ... (https://citizenportal.ai/articles/7768200/arizona/cochise-county/cochise-county-adopts-ordinance-to-regulate-data-centers-after-lengthy-public-hearing)\nciteturn3search20 [wordlim: 200] Why it matters: County staff said the rules are intended to ensure any future data-center proposal undergoes a site-specific review rather than being processed ...--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCochise County Open Data (https://gis-cochise.opendata.arcgis.com/)\nciteturn3search21 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; This site was built with ArcGIS Hub and uses anonymous tracking to improve performance and analyze site usage in accordance with Esri's Privacy Statement.Read more--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProposed data center prompts Tucson to regulate large water users, require conservation (https://apnews.com/article/42c1e554b02b4293685a08a4574db9f0)\nciteturn3news22 [wordlim: 100] Published: last year; In response to mounting public concern over water use by a proposed data center project called “Project Blue,” the Tucson City Council has unanimously passed a new ordinance regulating access to Tucson’s water system for large-scale users. The ordinance requires any entity expecting to use over 7.4 million gallons of water per month to apply for water service, provide detailed water conservation plans, and receive council approval. All applications will be treated as public records, enhancing transparency.\n\nPublic opposition, driven by secrecy and environmental concerns around Project Blue, ultimately led the council to reject its annexation proposal. The ordinance introduces penalties for excessive water use and mandates periodic updates of conservation plans. However, some critics argue that the regulation does not go far enough, especially regarding data centers, and advocates are urging for broader restrictions on large water and power-intensive projects.\n\nThe Pima County Board of Supervisors is also initiating efforts to impose zoning controls on industrial water users. Tucson has a history of water conservation leadership, and the ordinance demonstrates its continued commitment to sustainable growth amid increasing climate concerns. A six-month public review period will follow the ordinance’s implementation for further input and revisions.\nProposed data center prompts Tucson to regulate large water users, require conservation\nDate: Thu Aug 21 16:38:56 2025 UTC\nLocation: Tucson, Arizona, United States\n\nTUCSON, Ariz. (AP) - Until this week, a company with zoning approval in Tucson could have relatively unlimited access to Tucson's water system, even if it was going to use millions of gallons of water.\nNow, with the unanimous passage of a new ordinance by the city council, any large water user that wants to gain access to Tucson's water will have to apply to the city and show its water conservation efforts. \nThe goal, said Mayor Regina Romero, was to protect the city from large water users like data centers as quickly as possible. \"Approving this tonight will help protect us immediately,\" Romero said Tuesday. \nThe ordinance comes after weeks of a heady and impassioned debate over Project Blue, a massive and secretive data center proposal that could have used hundreds of millions of gallons of water a year. \nTo get those gallons, the project had to be annexed into Tucson - a step that also brought its massive water use before the Tucson City Council for review. \nProposed data center prompts Tucson to regulate large water users, require conservation\nDate: Thu Aug 21 16:38:56 2025 UTC\nLocation: Tucson, Arizona, United States\n\nA wave of reform \n\nThe Pima County Board of Supervisors voted Monday to initiate a process to potentially add zoning restrictions on large-scale industrial users, with a specific intent to add guardrails to any data center that wants to build in the region. \nIf implemented, the new process would require additional public engagement and official review. Supervisor Jen Allen, of District 3, who introduced the measure, said it was important to work in tandem with the city of Tucson as they begin their own process of establishing a more thorough review process for proposed data centers. \nAllen also said that other jurisdictions in Arizona and across the country have already imposed similar guidelines. Allen said \"30 or more\" data centers have their sights on Pima County.\nTucson's ordinance is also not the last word on the large water user rules. The ordinance would then be subject to a six-month public review period, including from the Citizen Water Advisory Committee, to consider adjustments, said Ward 6 council member Karin Uhlich in her newsletter.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPhoenix City Council to consider zoning policy for data centers (https://www.axios.com/local/phoenix/2025/05/13/phoenix-city-council-data-centers-zoning)\nciteturn3news23 [wordlim: 100] Published: 1.3 years ago; The Phoenix City Council is considering implementing zoning regulations to control the locations of data centers within the city due to concerns over their high energy use, space demands, and potential for noise pollution. The proposal aims to restrict data centers to industrial and select commercial zones, steering them away from mixed-use, pedestrian-friendly, and transit-oriented neighborhoods. This move reflects growing awareness of the impacts of these facilities, which are vital for tech companies and services like artificial intelligence. Arizona currently hosts 164 data centers, predominantly in the Valley region. The city’s planning commission will review the proposal on June 5, with a City Council vote expected on June 18. This initiative follows Chandler’s pioneering zoning law for data centers established in 2022. Additionally, the Arizona Corporation Commission recently initiated a review of data center policies in light of their significant energy consumption.\nPhoenix City Council to consider zoning policy for data centers\nDate: Tue May 13 13:20:00 2025 UTC\n\nPhoenix City Council to consider zoning policy for data centers\n\nPhoenix is on the verge of enacting zoning regulations to limit where data centers can be located.\nWhy it matters: Data centers use large amounts of energy and space and can bring negative effects like noise pollution to surrounding communities, per an analysis by city staff.\nState of play: The city wants to direct data centers away from mixed-use, walkable and transit-oriented communities.\nZoom in: Under the proposal, data centers would be restricted to areas zoned for industrial and some commercial use.\nThe big picture: Data centers house computer equipment for data storage and are essential for companies including Amazon and Google and technologies such as artificial intelligence.\nBy the numbers: There are 164 data centers in Arizona, mostly in the Valley, according to research firm Baxtel.\nWhat's next: The city's planning commission will hear the proposed policy on June 5, and the City Council is expected to vote on it June 18, Gomes said.\nFlashback: Chandler became the first Valley city to enact zoning for data centers in late 2022, the Arizona Republic reported.\nWhat we're watching: The Corporation Commission last month opened a review on data center policies to find a way to cope with their extensive energy use.\n"
